  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\u27e8\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1\u27e9$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-y \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1\u27e9\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $\u27e88,2\\pi ,2\u27e9.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot \u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1\u27e9=\u27e88,2\\pi ,2\u27e9.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-cy=8 \\\\ -xc=2\\pi  \\\\ -c=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,\u27f9\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,\u27f9\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$",
